/*!
\internal
\title Qt Quick 3D Mesh File Format
\page quick3d-mesh-format

This describes the binary layout of the \c .mesh files that
\l{Balsam Asset Import Tool}{Balsam} writes and that \c Model::source loads. It
documents what the reader in \c qssgmesh.cpp accepts, so that the parser, the
writer and any external tooling can be checked against one description.

\section1 Container

A \c .mesh file is a container holding one or more meshes, each with its own
vertex and index buffers and subset views into them. It is read back to front:
the last 16 bytes are the footer, the entry list sits immediately before it, and
each entry gives the absolute offset of one mesh.

All values are little endian. Floating point values are 32-bit. Some strings are
UTF-8 and some are UTF-16LE, as noted per field.

\badcode
[mesh 0][mesh 1]...[entry 0][entry 1]...[footer]
\endcode

\section2 MultiMeshFooter, 16 bytes, at the end of the file

\table
\header
    \li Type
    \li Field
    \li Notes
\row
    \li quint32
    \li fileId
    \li 555777497
\row
    \li quint32
    \li version
    \li 1, the only container version
\row
    \li quint32
    \li entriesOffset
    \li Ignore, see \l{Offset fields}
\row
    \li quint32
    \li entriesSize
    \li Number of meshes, and so of entries
\endtable

\section2 MultiMeshEntry, 16 bytes, entriesSize of them before the footer

\table
\header
    \li Type
    \li Field
    \li Notes
\row
    \li quint64
    \li meshOffset
    \li Absolute offset of the mesh. The only offset in the format that can be
        trusted
\row
    \li quint32
    \li meshId
    \li Identifies the mesh within the container
\row
    \li quint32
    \li padding
    \li Unused
\endtable

\section1 Mesh

\section2 MeshDataHeader, 12 bytes, at meshOffset

\table
\header
    \li Type
    \li Field
    \li Notes
\row
    \li quint32
    \li fileId
    \li 3365961549
\row
    \li quint16
    \li fileVersion
    \li 3 to 7, see \l{Versions}
\row
    \li quint16
    \li headerFlags
    \li Unused
\row
    \li quint32
    \li sizeInBytes
    \li Size of the mesh that follows, excluding this header
\endtable

\section2 Mesh, 56 bytes

Fourteen quint32 fields. Three of them were repurposed in version 7, which is
why the size did not change:

\table
\header
    \li Field, before version 7
    \li Field, version 7
    \li Notes
\row
    \li vertexBuffer.entriesOffset
    \li targetBufferEntriesCount
    \li Ignore before version 7
\row
    \li vertexBuffer.entriesSize
    \li vertexBuffer.entriesSize
    \li Number of vertex buffer entries
\row
    \li vertexBuffer.stride
    \li vertexBuffer.stride
    \li Bytes per vertex
\row
    \li vertexBuffer.dataOffset
    \li targetBufferDataSize
    \li Ignore before version 7
\row
    \li vertexBuffer.dataSize
    \li vertexBuffer.dataSize
    \li Bytes of vertex data
\row
    \li indexBuffer.componentType
    \li indexBuffer.componentType
    \li See \l{Component types}
\row
    \li indexBuffer.dataOffset
    \li indexBuffer.dataOffset
    \li Ignore
\row
    \li indexBuffer.dataSize
    \li indexBuffer.dataSize
    \li Bytes of index data, zero for a non-indexed mesh
\row
    \li subsetsOffset
    \li targetCount
    \li Ignore before version 7
\row
    \li subsetsSize
    \li subsetsSize
    \li Number of subsets
\row
    \li jointsOffset
    \li jointsOffset
    \li Ignore
\row
    \li jointsSize
    \li jointsSize
    \li Number of joints, see \l{Joints}
\row
    \li drawMode
    \li drawMode
    \li See \l{Draw modes}
\row
    \li winding
    \li winding
    \li See \l{Windings}
\endtable

\section2 Blocks after the Mesh struct, in file order

\list 1
\li Vertex buffer entries, \c entriesSize of them, 16 bytes each: a
    \c nameOffset to ignore, then \c componentType, \c componentCount and the
    byte \c offset of the attribute within a vertex. Followed by padding.
\li Vertex buffer entry names, one per entry: a quint32 byte length, then that
    many bytes of UTF-8 including a terminating zero, then padding.
\li Vertex buffer data, \c vertexBuffer.dataSize bytes, then padding.
\li Index buffer data, \c indexBuffer.dataSize bytes, then padding.
\li Subsets, \c subsetsSize of them, sized by version, followed by padding.
\li Subset names, one per subset: \c nameLength UTF-16LE code units including a
    terminating zero, then padding.
\li Level of detail entries, in subset order, \c lodCount of them per subset,
    12 bytes each, followed by padding. Version 6 and later.
\li Joints, \c jointsSize of them, 136 bytes each.
\li Target buffer entries, names and data, laid out exactly as the vertex buffer
    ones. Version 7 only.
\endlist

\section2 Subset

40 bytes before version 5, 48 in version 5, 52 in version 6 and later:

\table
\header
    \li Type
    \li Field
    \li Notes
\row
    \li quint32
    \li count
    \li Number of indices, or of vertices when there is no index buffer
\row
    \li quint32
    \li offset
    \li First index, or first vertex
\row
    \li float[6]
    \li bounds
    \li Minimum xyz then maximum xyz
\row
    \li quint32
    \li nameOffset
    \li Ignore
\row
    \li quint32
    \li nameLength
    \li UTF-16 code units including the terminating zero, so the name occupies
        twice this many bytes
\row
    \li quint32
    \li lightmapWidth
    \li Version 5 and later
\row
    \li quint32
    \li lightmapHeight
    \li Version 5 and later
\row
    \li quint32
    \li lodCount
    \li Version 6 and later
\endtable

\section2 Level of detail entry, 12 bytes

A quint32 \c count and \c offset into the index buffer, as for a subset, and a
float \c distance, the screen space threshold at which the level is used.

\section1 Padding

Every block is aligned to four bytes, but not in the way that phrase usually
means. The alignment step adds \c{4 - (byteCounter % 4)} bytes, which is a full
four bytes when the position is already aligned. So an already aligned block
still carries four bytes of padding.

\note This started as a bug in the original writer, and became part of the
format the moment files were shipped with it. A reader that skips the padding
only when it is needed will misparse every file.

The padding follows the vertex buffer entries as a group, not each entry; the
same is true of the subsets and of the level of detail entries. Entry names and
subset names are each padded individually.

\section1 Offset fields

Several fields hold an offset that the original implementation filled in while
parsing, and which was then written back out. They carry no useful information
and differ between writers: version 3 files have whatever the producing tool
left there, and version 4 and later write zero. Read them and discard them.

\c MultiMeshEntry::meshOffset is the exception, and is the only offset a reader
should act on.

\section1 Joints

The 136 byte joint records hold a joint id, a parent id and two 4x4 matrices.
Qt Quick 3D does not use them, and its writer always emits \c{jointsSize = 0},
but the block is part of the layout: a reader must skip \c{jointsSize * 136}
bytes or everything after it will be misparsed. Only files from Qt 3D Studio
are known to carry joints.

\section1 Versions

The container version is 1 and has never changed. Mesh versions:

\table
\header
    \li Version
    \li Change
\row
    \li 1, 2
    \li Not supported. Unique to Nvidia Drive Design and early Qt 3D Studio
\row
    \li 3
    \li The oldest supported version, and the last that Qt 3D Studio produces
\row
    \li 4
    \li The offset fields are written as zero
\row
    \li 5
    \li Subsets gain a lightmap size hint
\row
    \li 6
    \li Subsets gain levels of detail
\row
    \li 7
    \li Morph target data moves to its own buffer, and three fields of the Mesh
        struct are repurposed to describe it. Written by current versions
\endtable

Before version 7 morph targets were extra vertex buffer entries named
\c attr_tpos0, \c attr_tnorm1 and so on, with anything beyond the supported
number named \c attr_unsupported. The reader converts these into a version 7
target buffer on load, so callers see one representation.

\section1 Enumerations

\section2 Component types

\table
\header
    \li Value
    \li Type
    \li Size
\row
    \li 1, 2
    \li quint8, qint8
    \li 1
\row
    \li 3, 4
    \li quint16, qint16
    \li 2
\row
    \li 5, 6
    \li quint32, qint32
    \li 4
\row
    \li 7, 8
    \li quint64, qint64
    \li 8
\row
    \li 9
    \li 16-bit float
    \li 2
\row
    \li 10
    \li 32-bit float
    \li 4
\row
    \li 11
    \li 64-bit float
    \li 8
\endtable

\section2 Draw modes

1 points, 2 line strip, 3 line loop, 4 lines, 5 triangle strip, 6 triangle fan,
7 triangles, 8 patches.

\section2 Windings

1 clockwise, 2 counter-clockwise.

\section2 Attribute names

\c attr_pos, \c attr_norm, \c attr_uv0, \c attr_uv1, \c attr_textan,
\c attr_binormal, \c attr_joints, \c attr_weights, \c attr_colors, and the
morph target names described under \l{Versions}.

\sa {Balsam Asset Import Tool}
*/
